inline.s:
The great matter of birth and death deserves the deepest grief;
Set to work at once — even now you are already late.
Only seek your liberation within your own mind;
There is no need to go searching anywhere beyond it.

The Buddha said: In the presence of Dipankara Buddha I received no prediction in truth;
if there had been a prediction, that would be clinging to a self.
Even in becoming a Buddha one still fears clinging to a self — how much more in the affairs of birth and death.

Only wish to empty yourself of all that you have, and never fill yourself with what you do not have.
These words are not the private heirloom of Old Pang alone;
all Buddhas and patriarchs are the same.

When deluded thinking arises, nirvāṇa appears;
when afflictions stir, the Buddha Way is accomplished.
This dharma may be known and seen only by one whose five eyes are perfect and bright.

I have often said that learning has three essentials.
It is said: without understanding the Spring and Autumn Annals, one cannot engage with the world;
without mastering Laozi and Zhuangzi, one cannot forget the world;
without practicing Chan, one cannot leave the world.
With these three, the learning of ordering the world and of leaving it is complete.
Lack one, and you are one-sided; lack two, and you are narrow.
Lacking all three and still calling oneself a man — that is merely to resemble one.
Yet one must not fail to know the essential. The essential is the root.
Therefore it is said: Words have a root, affairs have a lord.
If words have no root, they sprawl without order;
if affairs have no lord, they fragment and grow ever more tangled.
If learning has no essential, it scatters and accomplishes little.
Hence the student must by no means fail to pursue the essential.
Yet the essential of these three lies in the one mind.
The essential of tending the mind lies in practicing Chan;
the essential of practicing Chan lies in forgetting the world;
the essential of forgetting the world lies in suiting the time;
the essential of suiting the time lies in comprehending change;
the essential of comprehending change lies in seeing principle;
the essential of seeing principle lies in steadying the will;
the essential of steadying the will lies in being content with one's lot;
the essential of being content with one's lot lies in curbing desire;
the essential of curbing desire lies in knowing oneself;
the essential of knowing oneself lies in valuing the true self;
the essential of valuing the true self lies in attending to what is within;
the essential of attending to what is within lies in singleness of focus.
Gain the one, and the principle of all under heaven is gained.
Following principle while engaging with the world, nothing is not forgotten, nothing is not possessed.
Forgetting nothing and possessing nothing, then of things none is not forgotten, none is not possessed.
When of things none is forgotten and none is not possessed,
then wherever one enters, one is at ease.
Therefore it is said: Heaven and earth are born together with me, and all things are one with me.
To gather all things into oneself — is this not the sage alone?
Ah, how perfect and complete! The wondrous is exhausted in the one mind, and no affair is left out.
Therefore the student surely must not fail to know the essential.
